3
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.240
Build an event ledger before

4
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.740
calculating averages. Record arrivals, completion,

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.460
quantum expiry and wakeups separately,

6
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.540
using the tie convention stated

7
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.880
by the particular question.

8
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.220
For a CPU-only example, P

9
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.080
one through P four arrive

10
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.960
at zero, one, two and

11
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.660
four. Their bursts are six,

12
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.100
six, six and four, with

13
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.800
quantum two and zero switch

14
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.360
overhead. Existing waiters precede new

15
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.760
arrivals, which precede an unfinished

16
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.240
process rejoining after its expired

17
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:35.620
slice.

18
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.840
P one runs from zero

19
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.620
to two. At two, P

20
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.800
two is already waiting and

21
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.280
P three has just arrived,

22
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.560
so P one rejoins behind

23
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.340
them. The next turns are

24
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.700
P two from two to

25
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.500
four, P three from four

26
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.100
to six, then P one

27
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.180
from six to eight.

28
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.620
P four arrived at four

29
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.300
and now runs from eight

30
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.160
to ten. Continue the queue

31
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.400
mechanically: P two, P three,

32
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.200
P one, P four, P

33
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.640
two, then P three. The

34
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.900
completion times are sixteen, twenty,

35
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.300
twenty-two and eighteen in process

36
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:10.660
order.

37
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.200
Subtract arrivals to get turnaround

38
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.560
sixteen, nineteen, twenty and fourteen,

39
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.240
averaging seventeen point two five.

40
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.420
Response is zero, one, two

41
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.580
and four, averaging one point

42
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.580
seven five. With no blocking,

43
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.840
waiting is turnaround minus burst,

44
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.800
averaging eleven point seven five.

45
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.880
For the fork and message

46
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.260
worksheet, each executed line costs

47
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.140
one unit and the quantum

48
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.660
is two. The parent assigns

49
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.100
at zero and forks during

50
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.800
one to two, so the

51
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.020
child enters at two. That

52
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.120
child then attempts receive and

53
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.180
blocks at three.

54
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.560
The parent sends during three

55
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.120
to four and continues to

56
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.580
a receive that blocks at

57
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.700
five. The child runs its

58
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.000
add from five to six

59
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.680
and its send from six

60
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.380
to seven. Under the worksheet

61
00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.700
explicit wakeup-before-requeue rule, the parent

62
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.260
runs its exit from seven

63
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.180
to eight, then the child

64
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.720
exits from eight to nine.

65
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.480
The child turnaround is nine

66
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.680
minus its arrival two, giving

67
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.840
seven; the parent turnaround is

68
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.260
eight. Do not charge the

69
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.660
child for time before it

70
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.060
existed. A nonblocking send does

71
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.920
not automatically yield, while an

72
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.600
empty receive can create an

73
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.840
immediate scheduling event.

74
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.460
Some photo exercises include the

75
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.840
running process at the head

76
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.840
of the displayed ready queue.

77
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.560
That notation differs from an

78
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.760
implementation queue containing only waiting

79
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.440
runnable processes. Write the convention

80
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.960
beside your ledger and do

81
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.200
not mix the two halfway

82
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:38.600
